\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 I'm going to give you the full breakdown year by year for the seasons so that we can really build this out in a robust way. I don't know if you want to use all of this in each season, but you can use the draft of all of this and the background for all of this in other parts of the website as well as needed. This story starts in December of 2021, right around the time of my 49th birthday. The 49th running of the Honolulu Marathon was happening, and it was running not far from where we live in Hawaii. We were outside for a walk that morning, and we were watching all the runners participate in the Honolulu Marathon. It was just a really beautiful Sunday morning. Michelle said, "Hey, we should do this next year for your 50th birthday." Now, mind you, we had never run more than 8.1 miles at any one time before. We had done the Great Oahu Run on occasion in the past, not every year, just every other year or so. And we participated in a 5K or a 10K here and there, but really didn't enjoy them. With us both being in the army, we did a lot of running, but really hated running, to be honest. I was a fast runner in my early years in the army, and people used to ask me, "Why do I run so fast?" I said, "Because why would you want to run longer than you have to? The faster I run, the sooner I can get done with it." But in that moment of weakness, thinking about running a marathon a year away, it didn't seem like anything to really be too overly concerned about, and I just said, "Yeah, let's do it. Let's check that off our bucket list. Let's do the marathon for my 50th birthday." We made an agreement with each other that we would go ahead and get signed up for it, which we did a couple of days later, when they opened up the marathon signups for locals here in Hawaii called 'kama'aina' And now that it was on a calendar, it was serious. We decided, "Okay, we've got this big, scary event coming up on the calendar. It's a year away, but we've had friends that have done the marathon before, and they'd be wrecked for a week or two afterward. We didn't want that for us."\
\
We decided that we were going to train enough so that we could actually run the Honolulu Marathon, not walk the whole thing, but actually run it and have a great experience. We wanted to enjoy the experience and be one and done with the marathon. That was our plan going into this. In January we started running a little bit here and there: 1.5 miles one day, 2 miles the next, and we incorporated walking into our routine. We would walk Monday, Wednesday, and Friday for about 45 minutes to an hour just to break up the running. We would run 4 days a week and we would walk 3 days a week. And we would mix it up. We would run some hills. We would run fast one day, slow the next but we really didn't have any idea what we were doing. We were doing it and as the time went on it just got better and better and easier and we were able to increase the distances. We actually had a vacation around Michelle's birthday in April of 2022, where, for the first time ever, we packed running shoes and went to Florida for a golf vacation. We actually ran twice while we were on this trip for 10 days, which was amazing because I would have never ever gone on vacation and gone for a run before ever. It was so funny too because we had a great story from that trip. We had played golf one day and at some of the golf courses they have the restrooms that have a keycode entry. The next day we were doing a run and we were running through the same neighborhood that was near the golf course that we had just played the day before. Michelle really had to go to the bathroom and there were these porta potties because the neighborhood was under construction. There were these porta potties strewn about and she didn't want to use any of those. We came across one of the golf course restrooms with the key code. Because I've always been really good with numbers, I remembered the key code from the day before when we used it, and to this day we never forget. I hope they changed it but the code was 3812. Anyway there came a point during this running. I don't know if it's the runner's high or whatever it is. As I had mentioned before, we had always hated running but there was a time where we realized that instead of dreading going out for a run, we actually dreaded if we missed a run. That's when we knew that we were addicted. That was the moment we knew that we were hooked. Still running sucks. Running is hard. Running is painful but there was something to it that just kept us coming back and something that just felt terrible about missing it. It's really very, very difficult to explain. But it's when you do the running and you exhaust yourself that the dopamine hit you get afterwards is just really fantastic. As bad as running is, the feeling that you get, the reward that you get afterwards, makes it all worthwhile. \
\
The following month in May of 2022 was going to be our very first half marathon, called the Hibiscus Half Marathon. It actually runs through part of the course for the Honolulu Marathon and the last 6 miles of the Hibiscus Half Marathon or the last 6 miles of the Honolulu Marathon. It was a good warm-up for what we're getting ourselves into. Up until that point our training had taken us on runs of 7, 8, and 8.5 miles but I don't think we went over 8.5 or 9 miles in our training for that first half marathon. I remember we ran that half marathon together. And came in right around 2.5 hours, which is not too shabby considering it is our very first one. And it was a really great experience running with all those other couple hundred runners out there on that beautiful Sunday morning. It was just a really enjoyable experience. \
\
What we did next is, over the summer of 2022, we started to work in a little bicycling and swimming every now and then, just to break up all of the running that we were doing all week long and give our joints a break. And we signed up for a sprint triathlon at the end of July 2022 called the Nawahine Triathlon. It was the first year that they opened it up to men also. And it was a short triathlon: a 500 m swim, a 12-mile bike, and then a 5K run but it was really cool. It was just a lot of fun and we kept the rotation of bicycling and swimming as part of our weekly routines just to add some variety to our training. \
\
Then, we bought a book from Amazon called "Running Your First Marathon: The Complete 20-Week Marathon Training Plan" By a gentleman named Andrew Kastor. And when we were just about 20 weeks out from the marathon in July, we started following his training plan for the remainder of the year and we had a couple of races built in.  Back then it was the BioAstin Marathon Readiness Series that we signed up for. They had:\
