\cf0 There were five series of races, which they got the runners prepared for the marathon by competing in these ever-increasing events. The farthest we ran leading up to the Honolulu Marathon was the 30K, which is 18.6 miles. By the time the end of November came, our training was pretty much done and it was time to taper into the marathon, which was on December 11, 3 days after my 50th birthday. And my parents flew out from South Carolina to not only celebrate my 50th birthday but also to watch us compete in our first marathon. That day we got out there bright and early. We caught the shuttle down to Magic Island, Ala moana Beach Park, where the Honolulu Marathon attendees gather, and there were about 25,000 people there on the street, ready and willing to go. Some of them were doing the start-to-park, which is the 10K. There were probably about 15,000 people doing the 10K and 10,000 people doing the marathon but we all gathered together for the start. The Honolulu Marathon starts at 5 am, so really early, because once the sun gets up it gets really hot even in December. It turned out to be a beautiful day with beautiful conditions and we both ran our own race that day. Michelle came in at 5:42 and I came in at 4:22. My goal was to come in under 4:30 and I beat my goal. Michelle's goal was to come in under 6:00 and she beat her goal. We both ran the entire race and just had a really great experience. Grabbed a couple of malasadas at the finish area there and just really enjoyed the day. \
\
What did it cost? Well it was a whole year of training up for that but we learned a lot about ourselves that year. We learned that running can be a way of life, and there is a runner's high, and running does feel good. You just have to do it long enough until your body switches that on. We learned that you can't really run past 9 miles consistently on a keto diet. We had been practicing keto for about 3.5 years and in the summer of 2022, when we really started to increase the mileage for our runs, we realized that you do need carbs. And so we started introducing carbs back into our diet. Nothing crazy. We would just increase our fruit intake and we started eating potatoes more often and rice occasionally. But it was enough to make sure that we sustained the amount of energy that we needed for our runs. And we also learn that the longer you run, you do have to start eating while you're running. Nutrition is a whole other rabbit hole that we've gone down and are still going down as we're still trying to fine-tune our training and our race nutrition, as it is paramount for endurance training. After the marathon we had a post-race letdown where we're like, "Well now what?" We had done all these races, we'd done all these events, and we just completed the marathon. We're thinking that's it, we're one and done, but we were like, "Man we just need something more. We need something on the calendar." And it was over Christmas of 2022, two weeks after the marathon, that we decided, "You know what? Let's do it again next year. Let's put the marathon back on the calendar and give ourselves something to train for." That way we can just keep doing this and stay in shape. And then I decided I wanted to take it one step further and I said, "Hey Chelle, why don't we sign up for the Hawaii 70.3 over on the Big Island?" And she thought I was crazy. She said, "No way, crazy man! You sign up for that thing yourself." So I did and talked a couple of my crazy friends into signing up for it as well. Well that pretty much closes out 2022. \
